.TH CYAML_PARSE_MP 3 "2026-09-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_parse_mp, cyaml_parse \- parse a null-terminated YAML string into a DOM tree
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cyaml.h>

cyaml cyaml_parse_mp(const char *yaml_str, char **err_str,
                     ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
cyaml cyaml_parse(const char *yaml_str, char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Parse a null-terminated YAML string and return the root DOM node.
Comments (#) are silently discarded.  Anchors (&name) and aliases
(*name) are resolved to independent deep clones.  Implicit YAML 1.2
core schema typing is applied to unquoted scalars.

A tag (!tag, !!tag, !<verbatim>, or a %TAG-directive shorthand) is
resolved and attached to its node; see cyaml_node_tag(3) for how a
tag interacts with a node's type.  A "<<" mapping key written as a
plain, untagged scalar is expanded as a merge key; see the Merge
keys section of README.md for the expansion and precedence rules.
A tag/content type mismatch (!!null excepted: it forces CYAML_NULL
unconditionally, on any text, since there is no canonical "wrong"
spelling for it), a malformed %TAG directive, or an invalid
merge-key source is a parse failure, exactly like any other
malformed input.

SINGLE-DOCUMENT STREAMS
When the input contains exactly one document (with or without a leading
"---" marker and an optional trailing "..." marker), the root node of
that document is returned directly.

MULTI-DOCUMENT STREAMS
When the input contains multiple documents separated by "---" markers,
all document roots are collected and returned as a single CYAML_LIST
node whose elements are the individual document roots in order.  The
anchor table is reset between documents so an anchor defined in one
document cannot be referenced by a later document.  A later document
may omit its own "---" marker only when the document immediately
before it ended with an explicit "..." marker; otherwise it must begin
with "---".  Bare content directly following a document with no
"..." is rejected as a parse error.  A document that begins with its
own "%YAML" directive always requires an explicit "..." before it,
even when it supplies its own "---" marker.

cyaml_parse() is an inline wrapper that passes NULL for mp.

On parse failure *err_str (if non-NULL) normally receives a
heap-allocated, null-terminated error message, allocated through mp,
that the caller must free with cyaml_serialize_free_mp() (passing the
same mp); this is a safe no-op if *err_str is NULL.  On success
*err_str is set to NULL.  *err_str may itself remain NULL even on
failure if allocating the message text also fails (only reachable
under an already-exhausted custom allocator); the return value, not
*err_str, always indicates success or failure.  Pass NULL for err_str
to discard error details.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
yaml_str   Null-terminated YAML input.
err_str    Out-parameter for the error string (allocated through mp;
           free with cyaml_serialize_free_mp()), or NULL to ignore.
mp         Custom allocator for all nodes in the resulting tree, or
           NULL for the default allocator.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
For a single-document input: the root cyaml node.
For a multi-document input: a CYAML_LIST node wrapping all document
roots.
NULL on parse failure or OOM.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The caller owns the returned tree and must free it with cyaml_destroy().

To distinguish single- from multi-document results, check the type of
the returned node with cyaml_type().

A plain, single-quoted, or double-quoted scalar VALUE may span multiple
physical lines, folded to a single space per line break (consecutive
blank lines fold to that many newlines instead), matching a folded
block scalar's own folding rules.  An implicit mapping KEY ("key:",
with no leading "?"), by contrast, must always fit on a single
physical line regardless of scalar style; an explicit key ("? key")
has no such restriction.  A sequence, mapping, or flow collection used
directly as a dictionary key is canonicalized to its flow-style text
representation.  A scalar dictionary key's own core-schema type is
also resolved before it is stored, exactly like an ordinary value
position ("~", "null", and an empty key all canonicalize to the
identical "null" string key); this applies uniformly to an implicit
key, an explicit key, a flow dictionary key, and a flow sequence's
"key: value" shorthand, so every spelling of the same value collides
on the same key regardless of notation.  A quoted key is never
subject to this: its stored key text is always exactly its own
decoded content.

A node may carry at most one anchor and one tag (in either order); an
alias may never itself carry either.  A tag decorating a dictionary
key is parsed for validity but discarded, since this DOM's keys are
plain strings rather than nodes.  A "<<" key is treated as a merge
directive whenever it is a plain, untagged scalar, in any mapping
regardless of how that mapping arose: a block mapping's implicit
("<<: value") or explicit ("? <<" / ": value") style; a flow
mapping's implicit ("{<<: value}") or explicit ("{? <<: value}")
style; or the single-entry mapping produced by a block or flow
sequence's own "key: value" / "? key: value" compact-mapping
shorthand ("- <<: value", "[<<: value]", "[? <<: value]"); and
regardless of whether the key itself carries an anchor.  A quoted or
explicitly tagged "<<" is always an ordinary literal key in every one
of those forms.

A raw, unescaped C0 control byte (0x00-0x1F other than tab) or DEL
(0x7F) is a parse error wherever it appears literally in scalar
content, in every scalar style; a double-quoted scalar's own escape
sequences (e.g. "\ex01", "\ex7f", "\e0") remain the only way to embed
one of these bytes in a string value.

Input is otherwise treated as an opaque byte string: no byte is
checked for well-formed UTF-8, so a byte sequence that is not valid
UTF-8 is accepted as literal scalar content and reproduced unchanged
by cyaml_serialize(3)/cyaml_serialize_flow(3). Feed well-formed UTF-8
text if a downstream consumer of the resulting strings requires it.

An implicit decimal, hex, or octal integer literal whose value does
not fit in a signed 64-bit long long falls back to CYAML_FLOAT (or,
failing that, a plain CYAML_STRING) rather than being silently
reinterpreted as a wrapped-around or wrong-signed integer.

A tab character is never valid as block-structural indentation or as
the separator directly after a block sequence ('-') or explicit-key
('?') indicator.  Block-structured content (a mapping or sequence)
must begin on its own line following an explicit "---" marker, not
share that line; a scalar or flow collection may share it.

A document nesting mappings, sequences, or explicit keys more than
500 levels deep is rejected with a parse error.  A non-scalar
dictionary key whose canonicalized flow-YAML text exceeds 64 KiB is
also rejected; this bounds a key that is itself a mapping containing
another such key many levels deep, since canonicalizing it re-quotes
each enclosing level's own already-quoted text, growing rapidly with
nesting depth.  Neither limit is reachable by any realistic
hand-written or generated document.

A single parse call also rejects a document that would require
allocating more than 4,000,000 DOM nodes in total, with a parse
error naming the limit explicitly; reachable only by a document with
millions of distinct scalar values, an extent no realistic
hand-written or generated document approaches.
.fi
